.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# Engagement notes name a customer, their people, their scope and their timelines. THIS REPO IS
# PUBLIC, and such notes are not toolkit content - they belong in the session workspace, never here.
# (Learned the hard way: a workshop plan and a prerequisites email were committed and pushed before
# anyone noticed the repo was public.)
#
# `**/` not `/migrations/`: these three were root-anchored at DEPTH 1 (`/migrations/customer-*/`),
# but the user trees are two levels deep (`migrations/workbooks/<slug>/`,
# `migrations/datasources/<slug>/`), so prefixing a slug - the documented opt-out for a migration
# that must stay local - matched NOTHING and tracked the whole unit, `fabric/**` (TMDL partitions
# with server/database names, M queries, PBIR) and `migration-spec.json` included. Measured before
# the fix: `migrations/customer-foo/...` IGNORED, `migrations/workbooks/customer-foo/...` NOT
# IGNORED (issue #378). The identical trap is written up four lines below for `migration-brief.md`;
# it was fixed there and not generalised to its own neighbours. `**/` matches at every depth, so it
# subsumes the old depth-1 rules rather than sitting beside them.
#
# The trailing `/` is load-bearing: these ignore DIRECTORIES only. Dropping it would also swallow
# tracked files whose NAME starts with a prefix - `docs/customer-text-exposure.md` is exactly that.
# Pinned by tests/test_gitignore_privacy_prefixes.py.
**/workshop-*/
**/engagement-*/
**/customer-*/
# `**/` not `/migrations/*/`: the depth-1 rule did NOT cover the path AGENTS.md actually instructs -
# `migrations/workbooks/<slug>/migration-brief.md` is two levels deep, and `git check-ignore` on that
# exact documented path returned NOT IGNORED. A brief names the customer, their scope, their people
# and their destination workspace, so the rule has to match wherever a brief can legitimately land.
**/migration-brief.md

# The three migration trees share one shape (<tree>/<slug>/...), so these rules use `**/` to apply to
# all of them: examples/ (our worked examples), migrations/workbooks/ (your workbook migrations) and
# migrations/datasources/ (your published-data-source migrations).

# Source Tableau workbooks / data sources may contain customer data - never commit
**/source/*.twb
**/source/*.twbx
**/source/*.tds
**/source/*.tdsx
**/source/*.hyper
# `**/` not `<tree>/*/data/`: the user trees are one level deeper (migrations/workbooks/<slug>/data/),
# so a fixed-depth rule would silently stop ignoring extracted customer data.
**/data/
# `tableau_lineage.py --download` pulls LIVE published data sources off Tableau Server into
# migrations/datasources/_downloads/. Never commit those.
**/_downloads/

# Reference screenshots of the SOURCE Tableau dashboards ARE committed for OUR examples/ - those
# sources are public Tableau Public workbooks, and the before/after images are the whole point of
# the showcase. Keep them reasonably sized (downscale big infographic captures).
#
# YOUR trees are the opposite default: a screenshot of a real customer dashboard is customer data,
# and this is where such work lands, so they are ignored. Un-ignore them only if you have cleared
# it (CONTRIBUTING.md: never stage customer-identifiable screenshots).
/migrations/workbooks/*/reference/
/migrations/datasources/*/reference/
# Local-only scratch from the capture bundle, in every tree:
**/reference/_thumbnails/
**/reference/manifest.json

# Fabric/PBI build output can be regenerated - keep repo lean (comment out if you want to version it)
# **/fabric/

# Migration _work scratch - full-folder backups, Desktop screenshots, downloaded
# boundary files. The re-runnable *.py transform scripts in _work ARE tracked.
**/_work/backup-*/
**/_work/*.png
**/_work/*.geojson

# Build-time scratch renders (skeleton/actual comparison screenshots)
**/_scratch/
# Keep `_build/` scratch ignored, but track the portable tamper-audit trail:
# - replay scripts (`_build/*.py`)
# - declaration ledger (`_build/generated-edit-declarations.json`) and append-only records
**/_build/**
!**/_build/*.py
!**/_build/generated-edit-declarations.json
!**/_build/generated-edit-declarations/
!**/_build/generated-edit-declarations/*.json

# .NET build output from the TMDL validation helper C# projects - regenerated by
# `dotnet build`; only the Program.cs / *.csproj source belongs in git.
[Bb]in/
[Oo]bj/

# `harvest_tableau_public.py` output (`--out-dir`, default `_harvest`): Tableau Public downloads +
# parsed specs (bulk .twbx + specs, transient corpus for parser stress-testing / hard-workbook
# selection - never commit). `classify_harvest_hardness.py` reads `_harvest/specs` from here.
# GLOB, not an exact name: a second run under `_harvest-2`/`_harvest-2026-08-13` - the natural move
# once `_harvest/` is occupied - was NOT ignored, and staged .twbx on a PUBLIC repo (issue #125).
/_harvest*/
candidates.json

# assess_estate.py output. This is a REAL CUSTOMER ESTATE: workbook and project names,
# owner LUIDs, local group names and their membership counts, and per-object permission
# grants. None of it belongs on a public repo, and the default `--out` is not a temp dir,
# so ignore the conventional name rather than relying on everyone remembering.
/_assessment*/

# `harvest_estate_assets.py` output (`--out`): downloaded .twbx/.tdsx from a REAL site plus the parse
# sweep over them (`parse-sweep.json` carries every workbook and datasource NAME). Customer content -
# never commit. `_sweep` is THE convention for this tool; `_harvest*` above belongs to the OTHER
# harvester, and one folder documented for two tools is how a name collision starts (issue #125).
# GLOB for the same reason as above, so `_sweep2`/`_sweep-2026-08-13` are covered by default. The
# script itself now refuses to write to an unignored in-repo path, so this rule is enforced, not
# merely hoped for.
/_sweep*/

# run_estate.py output (the conventional `--output` name, and dated/suffixed variants). A bundle is
# the most customer-identifying artifact this toolkit produces: report.json carries every workbook
# and project name and every calculated-field formula, and pbip/ carries the generated TMDL + PBIR.
# Its siblings above were already ignored; this one was not, which is the more dangerous half.
/_bundle*/
/_estate*/

# THE GENERAL RULE, added after the five specific ones above proved the pattern does not scale.
# Each of those was a separate near-miss patched separately - `/_harvest*/` and `/_sweep*/` both cite
# issue #125, and `/_sweep*/`'s own comment says "one folder documented for two tools is how a name
# collision starts". Nobody ever generalised, so the next tool to invent a prefix was unprotected by
# construction. Measured 2026-08-24 on a customer machine: 14 `_*` roots plus loose `_probe_*.py` and
# an 11 MB `_calibration-*.jsonl` at the repo root, and a reflexive `git add -A` after a gapped pull
# staged 111 of them into a merge commit. `_probe_acmu` slipped past the existing `_probe-lab/`
# purely because one uses an underscore and the other a hyphen.
#
# Root-anchored and deliberately covers FILES as well as directories, because the loose files were
# the half nothing caught. ✅ Verified safe before adding: all 96 tracked underscore paths are NESTED
# (`examples/*/_work/`, `.github/skills/*/scripts/_*.py`); there are ZERO top-level `_*` tracked
# entries, so this cannot un-track anything that exists. If a root `_*` file ever needs committing,
# negate it explicitly (`!/_thing`) rather than deleting this rule.
#
# ⚠️ This is a BACKSTOP, not the control. It cannot protect an output that does not start with `_` -
# see the `ses-prep/` near-miss in issue #322, where a deliverable naming 17 real customer servers
# landed unprefixed and unignored. Scripts that emit customer-identifying data must refuse to write
# to an unignored path (`refuse_unignored_output`), not rely on someone choosing a lucky name.
/_*

# Power BI Desktop local cache/settings - machine-specific, regenerated automatically on open
**/.pbi/cache.abf
**/.pbi/localSettings.json
**/.pbi/editorSettings.json

.env
.env.local
*.log

# Ad-hoc test scaffolding. These exist to test the toolkit, not to ship with it: multi-model gate
# sweeps, throwaway Tableau fixtures, and test-account provisioning. They are hard-wired to one
# person's Databricks/Snowflake tenant (account ids, warehouse ids), so committing them puts private
# infrastructure identifiers in a public repo AND presents an end user with scripts that cannot work
# for them. Kept on disk, out of the repo.
scripts/gate_matrix.py
scripts/probe_lab.py
scripts/provision_snowflake_fixture.py
scripts/setup_snowflake_keypair.py
scripts/snowflake_fixture.sql
tests/test_gate_matrix.py
_matrix/

# Credential material. `.env` alone was not enough: a real secret arrived as a loose
# `PAT-snowflake-secret.txt` in the repo root and a `git add -A` staged it (caught before commit,
# 2026-08-02). These patterns make that class of accident impossible rather than merely unlikely.
secrets/
*secret*.txt
*secret*.json
*.pat
*.pem
*.key
*.p8
*.p12
*.pfx
*credential*.txt
!scripts/credential_gate.py

# Copilot CLI feedback bundles - session scratch, can be huge (1.5GB+); never commit
copilot-feedback-*.tgz

# Compiled/binary Power BI files - always commit the unpacked PBIP/TMDL/PBIR instead
*.pbix

# OS cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# uv lockfile - NOT committed on purpose. Running "uv lock" on a Microsoft-corp machine resolves against
# internal feeds (packagefeedproxy.microsoft.io / ms-feed-*.pkgs.visualstudio.com), which would leak
# internal infrastructure URLs and break "uv sync --frozen" for any external clone. The ">=" floors in
# pyproject.toml are the contract instead. To commit a lock, regenerate it against public PyPI first
# (UV_INDEX_URL=https://pypi.org/simple uv lock).
uv.lock

# Throwaway agent-behaviour test fixtures (see scripts/probe_lab.py).
# Kept although `/_*` above already covers it at the repo root: this pattern is UNANCHORED, so it
# also matches a nested `_probe-lab/` inside a fixture tree, which the root-anchored rule does not.
# (A second, duplicate copy of this line was removed in the same change.)
_probe-lab/

# Live multi-source test fixture: embeds real endpoint hostnames, so it is generated
# on demand by scripts/make_live_source_fixture.py and never committed. tests/test_live_multisource.py
# skips when it is absent, so a clean clone and CI stay green without it.
tests/fixtures/live/


# Tableau oracle capture - data-bearing (Tableau's computed values + rendered PNGs of a customer's
# dashboards). GLOB, not an exact name, for the same reason as `_harvest*` and `_sweep*` above
# (issue #125): a second run under `_oracle-2`/`_oracle-2026-08-19` was NOT matched by the exact
# `_oracle/` rule and would have been staged on this PUBLIC repo. Measured 2026-08-19:
# `git check-ignore _oracle-2/images/customer.png` exited 1 (not ignored) while `_oracle/...`
# exited 0. That second run is not hypothetical - `capture_tableau_oracle.py` rewrites
# `oracle-manifest.json` wholesale per invocation, and `docs/reference-capture.md` tells operators
# to archive the manifest between batch invocations, which makes `_oracle-2` the obvious next step.
/_oracle*/

# Copilot CLI crash dumps. `copilot.exe` writes these into the CWD - i.e. the repo root - as
# `report.<yyyymmdd>.<hhmmss>.<pid>.<n>.<nnn>.json`. Observed 2026-08-20: three host crashes in one
# session, each leaving one. They are untracked and one `git add -A` away from being committed to a
# PUBLIC repo, and they carry host process detail.
#
# READ IT BEFORE DELETING IT: the dump names the trigger (`"event": "Allocation failed - JavaScript
# heap out of memory"`, `"trigger": "OOMError"`) and timestamps the crash, which is the reference
# point the post-crash file-mtime forensics in AGENTS.md depend on.
#
# Anchored to the root AND requiring a digit after `report.`, deliberately: PBIR's
# `definition/report.json` is a critical committed artifact, and a loose `report.*.json` would be
# one careless edit away from shadowing it.
/report.[0-9]*.json

# Local large-refresh fixture outputs. Generated by scripts/make_refresh_fixture.py; never commit the
# CSV payload or the machine-specific Power Query SourceFolder binding.
/fixtures/large-refresh/data/
/fixtures/large-refresh/fabric/LargeRefresh.SemanticModel/definition/expressions.tmdl
